{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/714701-nefertiti-overdrive-over-the-top-kung-fu-in-pharaohic-egypt","id":714701,"title":"Nefertiti Overdrive - Over the Top Kung Fu in Pharaohic Egypt","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-22T09:43:23-07:00","id":714701,"title":"Nefertiti Overdrive - Over the Top Kung Fu in Pharaohic Egypt","content":"I made this thread in order to not derail <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/709334-19-10-15-monday-meeting-notes\">this other thread</a> concerning the imminent release of the backer pdf for Exalted 3e (*refreshes email* damn it). In it, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/709334-19-10-15-monday-meeting-notes?p=714124#post714124\">I mentioned I nearly got a heart attack</a> when this <i>other</i> RPG I had backed on Kickstarter decided to pick this day to send <i>their</i> corebook to their backers.<br /> <br /> The other game was called <i>Nefertiti Overdrive</i> by Fraser Ronald, and I described its setting a &quot;mythic kung fu Egypt&quot;. This garnered some interest, so I thought I'd write a little something about my first impressions of the book, after having read it cursorily. The PDF/PoD version will come out soon on DriveThruRPG.<br /> <br /> First of all, I apparently oversold it when I said &quot;mythic kung fu Egypt&quot;. The game's primary setting is assumed to be some form of historic-but-not-historically-accurate Pharaohic Egypt, with all the gold and opulence you'd care to indulge in, but also cinematic martial arts straight out of <i>Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon</i>. The setting does not seem to explicitly involve the type of mythical beasts and living gods I had expected, but in its defence, it shouldn't be too hard to insert them if one wished. Whether that is a good thing will be discussed below.<br /> <br /> Let's talk about style. This book is, very clearly, homebrew. I mean, it has a publishing company with a few other titles under its name, but what it amounts to seems to be this one guy making games in his spare time and apparently having skills and dedication enough that he gets some sold. That is by no means a condemnation, but it does come across clearly in the style the author uses. Ronald often addresses the reader directly and also recognises himself, speaking in terms of &quot;you&quot; and &quot;I&quot; in a casual, conversational tone. He comes across like someone sitting down by a table and explaining to you why this game he has right here is pretty cool, right, maybe we should play it some, don't you think? I think this speaks of his perspective: he is player first, author second, and the most important mission of the book is to facilitate play. That attitude is reflected in the book's layout.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, the system is light and abstract. From what I can understand, there is one real mechanic: formulate a few types of descriptors, assign a die type, and roll said die type when the descriptor becomes relevant. If you describe it in a cool manner (what I'd call a stunt, from Exalted), you get to use a better die. The character sheet is literally just a couple lines on a half-page where you can write your descriptors and some space for a backstory. Many things are arbitrarily decided by the GM (such as difficulties), and by the players (such as the next in turn to act; if I got it right, the one who acted last decides who gets to go next).<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, there's a similar situation with the setting. Here's a quote from page 8: &quot;'Wait', you say, 'I know nothing about Ancient Egypt!' '\u2018S all good', I say. 'Neither do I.'&quot; <i>Nefertiti Overdrive</i> does not concern itself with establishing a world. There is no insight into the overarching situation, no design tenets conveyed by the setting. It says &quot;you know all those images and ideas you have of what ancient Egypt was probably like? Do that, with kung fu!&quot;<br /> <br /> This is my main gripe with my first impression of the game. It doesn't seem designed. It seems to provide you with the bare bones of what you need: a problem resolution system and an idea of the time and space the players will be acting in, so that everyone is on the same page. But it doesn't actually do anything with that. It says it's &quot;high octane&quot; and &quot;cinematic&quot;, but nothing in the system or setting implies or supports that (other than possibly the stunt mechanic). In those respects, the book does not do much work.<br /> <br /> Where it does do work, however, is in the discussions on gameplay, table manners and the conduction of an actual adventure. The core book comes with a sample adventure and 13 pre-generated characters, ready to pick up and go. There are also rules and directions for making your own characters, and thorough discussion on GM practices and attitudes. These discussions involves outright stating that &quot;this game is not balanced&quot; and &quot;it can't be played <i>against</i> the GM; it just breaks down then&quot; (see for example the turn order example). A whole lot of the mechanics appear to be dependent on the goodwill of the players and GM to not ruin everyone's fun, and I suppose from that perspective, the most important thing does become discussing player and GM attitudes. He is frank and up-front about it, and I can't fault him for it.<br /> <br /> That said, I've never been a player type. I read RPGs, and imagine them, and talk about them on the internet. And for that purpose, <i>Nefertiti Overdrive</i> is frankly useless. I'd have been much better off buying a history book about ancient Egypt, or some book or movie set there. I backed it for 7 Canadian dollars for a pdf, and I'm not unhappy about the money, but I would have expected quite a bit more meat, so to speak. I'm happy I got the book, because it made me think in the lines of Egyptian fantasy, but I'm much more inclined to rip the idea put it on top of some more useful mechanics. Maybe Exalted 3e, if that turns out well (*refreshes email* damn it).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Dex Davican wrote:</b> I can say without exaggeration or dishonesty that I am the most creative man <i>ever to have lived</i>","edited":{"userid":75,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/75-weimann","user":"Weimann","date":". 10-22-2015, 12:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/75-weimann","name":"Weimann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar75_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1279"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-22T11:32:28-07:00","id":714835,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y8svNN8saeU\" target=\"_blank\">So long as it lets me play this out,</a> then it might be worth checking out. Or maybe I should just check out Mummy, instead?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/681-hand-of-omega","name":"Hand-of-Omega","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar681_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2747"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-22T16:06:26-07:00","id":716024,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hand-of-Omega</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/714701-nefertiti-overdrive-over-the-top-kung-fu-in-pharaohic-egypt?p=714835#post714835\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y8svNN8saeU\" target=\"_blank\">So long as it lets me play this out,</a> then it might be worth checking out. Or maybe I should just check out Mummy, instead?</div> </div> </div> </div>It makes me remember the time a friend and martial arts teacher that once told me of an &quot;sword-&amp;-sandal/wuxia in fantasy Egypt barabarian action flick&quot; he tried to make with some friends for fun, shangaing his students for the fit, muscled &amp; half-naked extras, anubis-head helmets and a weapon mixing Ankh and Sai, among lots of other tongue-in-cheek bits. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-22T16:57:21-07:00","id":716262,"title":"","content":"I actually quite like it. I'm probably not going to get a chance to play it any time soon, but I'd sure like to. The Sword's Edge Publishing games all have a crystal-clear high concept, and I like that a lot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a 